.docname {Architecture Overview}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Service | A program deployed on its own | `telemetry` |
    | Edge | The one door between the internet and the services | Caddy locally, Istio in the cluster |
    | Broker | A middleman that carries messages | Redis, Kafka |
    | Bounded context | One domain, as owned by one or more services | Alerting is `notification` |
    | Cedar | The language CrowdVision's access rules are written in | `policy.cedar` |
    | Tenant | One customer organization | Northfield |
    | Control plane | Services that run the platform, not the product | `registry`, `provisioner` |

Six [domains](../domain-model/domain-overview.qd) become twelve deployed parts. This page shows how, then how they run.

---

## From domain to services

| Domain | Services | Store |
|---|---|---|
| Identity & Access | Keycloak, `claims-gateway`, `tenancy`, `registry`, `provisioner` | PostgreSQL |
| Building Management | `digital-twin` | MongoDB |
| Telemetry Ingestion | `telemetry` | PostgreSQL + TimescaleDB |
| Telemetry Distribution | `dashboard`, `socket` | MongoDB, `socket` has none |
| Alerting | `notification` | MongoDB, Redis for the cooldown |
| Knowledge & Assistance | `chat`, `agent` | MongoDB, PostgreSQL + pgvector |

Plus `client`, the Vue app everyone uses.

Why Identity needs four services:

- `claims-gateway`: answers "who is this?" on every sign-in.
- `tenancy`: remembers organizations and memberships.
- `registry`: takes a new customer's signup.
- `provisioner`: turns a signup into a running organization.

A signup has no user yet, and a sign-in needs no new customer. They change for different reasons.

---

## The whole picture

.mermaid
    flowchart LR
        B["Browser"] --> E["Edge"]
        S["Sensors"] --> E
        E --> I["claims-gateway, tenancy"]
        E --> T["telemetry"]
        E --> Tw["digital-twin"]
        E --> C["chat, agent"]
        Tw == "Kafka" ==> T
        T == "Kafka" ==> N["notification"]
        T -. "Redis" .-> D["dashboard, socket"]
        N -. "Redis" .-> D

Thin arrow: a request. Dotted: Redis, may be lost. Thick: Kafka, kept. To keep it readable this shows the main paths only. [Communication & Data Flow](data-flow.qd) lists every one.

---

## Rules of the structure

| Rule | Reason |
|---|---|
| One database per service, never reach into another's | A model cannot leak through shared tables |
| Services keep no session | Who is asking travels with each request |
| One service, one domain | A change stays in one place |
| High volume goes over a broker | A slow reader never holds back a sensor |
| Every outbound HTTP call has a timeout | A hang gives no error, so no fallback runs |
| Shapes that cross a service live in `schemas/` | Two sides cannot drift apart unnoticed |
| The frontend keeps state in composables and components; a global store only when several screens share it | Less hidden state to reason about |

A change that breaks one of these is rejected, even with CI green. Rules for how a change is made: [Toolchain Overview](../toolchain/toolchain-overview.qd).

---

## Same idea, different word

The domain speaks plainly. The code uses older names.

| Domain | Code |
|---|---|
| Organization | Domain |
| Pass | JWT, `x-gateway-claims` |
| Limit | Threshold |
| Breach | `AlertEvent` |
| Focus | Building preferences, columns |
| Reading | One entry of a `TelemetryEnvelope` |

---

## Where code and domain disagree

The domain is right until someone decides otherwise. Each line is a to-do or a change to the domain.

| Domain says | Code does |
|---|---|
| One breach, one alert | One alert per breaching reading, muted for 300 s per room and field. A long breach alerts every 5 minutes |
| News: Breach Started, Breach Ended | No breach is kept, and nothing says it ended |
| Staff can edit rooms | Rooms are fixed at upload, there is no route to change them |
| Three roles | Four in `roles.json`: `admin` also exists |
| Starting Focus comes from Building Ready | `digital-twin` calls `dashboard` directly, best effort |

---

## Shared code

Never deployed, always built into a service.

| Folder | Holds | Used by |
|---|---|---|
| `backend/libs/auth-contracts` | Token shape, role ladder, HMAC signing | Go services |
| `backend/libs/auth-middleware` | Verify a JWT, or read the edge's header | `claims-gateway`, `tenancy` |
| `backend/libs/auth-policy` | The one Cedar bundle | Every service that runs Cedar |
| `schemas/*-schema` | Rust crates for shared shapes | Rust services |
| `schemas/fixtures`, `schemas/json` | Examples every language must parse, written contracts | All |

---

## Two ways to run it

| | Local | Cluster |
|---|---|---|
| Started with | `just stack dev` | `just k8s create` |
| Edge | Caddy | Istio gateway |
| Checks the token | `forward_auth` to `claims-gateway` | `RequestAuthentication` at the gateway |
| Between services | Plain, on Docker networks | mTLS, by `ztunnel` |
| Keycloak | Included | Not in these manifests |

Same paths, same gates. Differences are listed in [Trust & Service Mesh](service-mesh.qd).

---

## Routes

Same table on both edges.

| Path | Goes to | Gated |
|---|---|---|
| `/gateway` | `claims-gateway` | No: `client` has no token yet, it gets one here |
| `/tenancy` | `tenancy` | Yes |
| `/twin` | `digital-twin` | Yes |
| `/telemetry` | `telemetry` | Yes |
| `/telemetry/ingest`, `/collector`, `/observations` | `telemetry` | No, signed with HMAC instead |
| `/dashboard` | `dashboard` | Yes |
| `/notification` | `notification` | Yes |
| `/chat` | `chat` | Yes, streams answers as SSE |
| `/agent` | `agent` | No, see [Trust](service-mesh.qd) |
| `/socket.io` | `socket` | Yes |
| `/` | `client` | No |

`registry` and `provisioner` have no route: nothing outside may reach them.

.box {Exact paths} type:{warning}
    The three open `telemetry` paths are exact, and listed by hand on both edges. A sub-path falls under the gate and answers 401.
